mers alpha and beta tubulin so we can  purify the tubulin and from this  purified tubulin we can allow them to  polymerize in vitro and with  high-resolution microscopy and with high  resolution with high precision  microscopy we can follow the dynamics  over time and scientists have done these  experiments what they found is under  certain conditions the microtubules the  labeled microtubules what you can see in  this movie are not not at all stable  follow the arrow and you can appreciate  that the microtubules are growing and  shrinking over a point of time in a  cartoon manner we can understand that  several micro if we if we look at a time  frame microtubule would grow shrink and  change their morphology and change their  dynamics so this dynamic city which  exists exists in terms of microtubules  shortening and lengthening is known as  dynamic instability of microtubule so if  you track their motion over time and  calculate the length of the microtubule  in this time bout what we would see is  there are several events where  microtubule lengths dramatically  decreases we turn those event as  catastrophe  there are also events where there is a  recovery of the short of the microtubule  lane as if the microtubule length is now  gradually increasing and those about we  call it  rescue phase so these are the features  of the microtubule dynamics all these  things hint us that there could be a  critical critical concentration of these  tubulin dimers based on which  microtubule polymerization kinetics  could vary so there exists a critical  concentration for microtubule assembly  now if our given concentration which is  the concentration of tubulin present in  the solution is more than the critical  concentration then it would undergo  polymerization and increase in the  length of the microtubule whereas if the  concentration at a given time point is  less than the critical concentration  then microtubule would kind of t  polymerize and get destabilized and  short 